    "body": "<p>This Signature model designs a dawn chorus from first principles. A Signature chorus is specified as a list of virtual singers, each with a start time relative to sunrise, a centre frequency, a song duration, and a repetition rate, and the model mixes them into a schedule that shows when each voice is active. The design rule is the acoustic niche rule borrowed from the public edition: virtual singers are placed in different frequency bands and time slots so their songs overlap as little as possible, and the model reports the overlap that remains. The output is a schedule and a set of numbers, not audio, and any sound rendered from it is labelled SIMULATED. The model is useful for teaching: a student can move a singer&#x27;s band, watch overlap rise, and learn why real choruses organise themselves the way they do.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the model implements the acoustic niche hypothesis as a design rule: species in an undisturbed habitat tend to occupy distinct frequency bands and time slots. By encoding that rule as band and slot assignments, the model reproduces the qualitative pattern of a real dawn chorus, spread across bands with limited overlap, while remaining honest that it is a model: its singers are invented, its parameters are chosen, and its overlap figures describe the model, not a real woodland.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if 3 virtual singers each sing for 15 minutes inside a 60-minute window, total singing time is 3 x 15 = 45 minutes and the average number singing at once is 45 / 60 = 0.75. The demo prints total 45 minutes, average 0.75 simultaneous singers, and one singer&#x27;s share of the window, 15 / 60 = 25.0 percent.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Signature design study for this database, October 2026; acoustic niche hypothesis as described in the public edition</p>",
